{"type": "complete", "response": "I'm not capable of directly accessing or retrieving real-time system information, including CPU usage, available memory, or disk space. I can only provide general guidance and information.\n\nTo view the current system information, you can use various methods depending on your operating system:\n\n* On Windows, you can use the Task Manager (Ctrl + Shift + Esc) to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n* On Mac, you can use the Activity Monitor (Command + Space, then type \"Activity Monitor\") to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n* On Linux, you can use the `top` command or `htop` command in the Terminal to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n\nIf you need help with using any of these methods, I can provide more detailed instructions. However, I won't be able to access or view your system's current state directly.", "session_id": "default", "model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "model_key": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "model_provider": "omniroute", "model_fallback_used": false, "model_fallback_reason": "", "model_latency_ms": 3209, "model_ttft_ms": 2745, "intent": "conversation", "goal_id": "", "state": "", "executive": {"intent": "conversation", "message": "I'm not capable of directly accessing or retrieving real-time system information, including CPU usage, available memory, or disk space. I can only provide general guidance and information.\n\nTo view the current system information, you can use various methods depending on your operating system:\n\n* On Windows, you can use the Task Manager (Ctrl + Shift + Esc) to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n* On Mac, you can use the Activity Monitor (Command + Space, then type \"Activity Monitor\") to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n* On Linux, you can use the `top` command or `htop` command in the Terminal to view CPU usage, memory usage, and disk space.\n\nIf you need help with using any of these methods, I can provide more detailed